def decisions : List ImplementationDecision :=
  [ decision "logic-formula-ast"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reference", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "proof:Submitted logic never runs as code"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Add a pure module LeanFM.Logic with a closed inductive Formula: true, false, at(state), after(message), terminal, field(message, name, value), predicate(text), derived(name), not, and, or, implies, stronglyImplies, never, EX, AX, EF, AF, EG, AG, EU, AU, EW, AW, and an unsupported(operator) node for K, O, P, Pr and similar modal operators. Parse it from Lean.Json only; no constructor holds code, a path or a closure, so a submitted formula cannot run. Reject bare path operators X, F, G and U as typing errors naming the quantified alternatives. Bound input: 256 formula nodes, depth 64, 256-character strings and 64 KiB of arguments; exceeding an input bound yields LogicInputRejected with the bound named."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: parse every operator; reject code-shaped strings as atoms only, unknown keys, bare F/G/X/U, depth/node/string overflow; no IO in LeanFM.Logic (checked by the module having no IO definitions)"]
  , decision "logic-ctl-model"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Build a finite Kripke structure from a canonical task (looked up by id in the self-hosting spec) or an inline draft {states, initial, terminals, transitions[src,dst,message]}. A node is (task state, message just taken). Terminal states and deadlocked non-terminal states stutter on themselves so every path is infinite; deadlocks are reported. Evaluate by explicit fixpoint labelling over at most 4096 nodes; iteration is bounded by the node count so every check terminates. Verdicts: proved/disproved only when every atom is observable and the model fit the bound; unknown when a derived or predicate atom is present; unsupported when an unsupported operator is present; resource-limit when the model exceeds the node bound. Unknown state or message names are input errors, so typos are not silently false."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: AG/AF/EG/EF/EU/AU/EW/AW agree with LeanFM.CTL.holds on the self-hosting tasks; a 5000-state draft yields resource-limit not proved; predicate atom yields unknown; K yields unsupported"]
  , decision "logic-protocol"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Attack.lean"]
      "Patterns are LeanFM.Session.Attack.Pat parsed from JSON: a term, {var: name}, or pair, tuple, enc {key, body}, hash, pk and sk over patterns (pk and sk also take a plain name). Steps are {choose: {var, values}}, {fresh: var}, {send: {to, message}}, {recv: pattern} and {claim: {name, args}}; a role may declare sessions, 1 to 4, expanded by Attack.Role.sessions into threads named name, name#2, ... that each run the role once with their own bindings; at most 8 threads of 32 steps, value lists within 64 terms, patterns within 256 nodes. A send or claim whose variables no earlier choose, fresh or recv of its role binds is rejected, naming the role, step and variable. fresh x binds x to the atom x in session 1 and x#k in session k (Attack.freshName); role names and fresh variables may not contain #, no two roles make the same fresh variable, and a fresh value the attacker can already derive is rejected, so fresh values are distinct across sessions and new to the attacker. Attack.coverFresh adds to every domain that holds a fresh value its copies in the other sessions. An optional defender is Attack.Inline {name, protects, knows, drop, rewrite}: a receive by a protected thread (a declared role or its later sessions) takes any message of the expected shape or of a rewrite rule's match that the attacker can build, passes it through Inline.pass, and accepts the result if it matches the receive pattern (Pat.matchMsg). Inline.pass drops when a drop pattern matches, by Pat.matchSeen, any of visibleParts (pairs split, ciphertexts opened only when the defender can build the opening key from analz of its knows; a ciphertext it cannot open matches nothing, since encryption is randomised in practice); otherwise it rewrites a whole message matching a rewrite rule; otherwise it forwards. The delivery is a recv hop from the defender, and its draft transition carries no term, so the attacker learns nothing from it. Protocol.possibleClaims declares on the unreachable flags state every claim a claim step could make over its variables' candidate values, so a formula may name an unreachable claim while a claim no thread could make stays unknown. A defender named like a role or the attacker, protecting an undeclared role, or with a rewrite whose replacement uses a variable its match does not bind is rejected. Attack.Protocol.explore generates the model breadth-first up to 4096 states and marks it truncated if any state was left out; a truncated model answers resource-limit. Otherwise Protocol.draft writes it as a check_ctl draft (every message transition with from, to and term, every claim transition with its claim, the attacker's knowledge declared with seesAll) and check_ctl decides the formula; the answer keeps check_ctl's verdict, reading, mode and evidence, and adds the state and transition counts, the messages on the evidence path (Protocol.replayWire) as {event: send, from, to, via, term} for an honest send, naming its addressee as to and the attacker it went through as via, and {event: recv, from, to, term} for a delivery from the attacker, so the wire and the evidence name the same sender and addressee, and a note of the bounds. check_ctl gains the claimed(claim) atom: a draft transition may carry a claim; a node also holds the claims made on its path, so it is observable; a claim no transition makes is an unknown name."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: a defender dropping bad stops it in plaintext and passes good; bad sealed under pk(B) evades a defender without the key and is delivered by it; a defender holding sk(B) drops it and still delivers sealed good; a rewriting defender turns bad into good, shown in the evidence; undeclared protected roles, a defender named like the attacker and an inventing rewrite are rejected", "LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: Needham-Schroeder declared as JSON: secrecy and agreement disproved for 1978 with Lowe's messages on the wire, proved with Lowe's fix, an honest run still possible; an unbound variable in a send is rejected; an attacker named like a role is rejected; an unknown claim is rejected. NeedhamSchroeder.lean: the same verdicts from the Lean declaration"]
  , decision "logic-secrets"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.secrets", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "ProtocolTool.secretsOf collects the protocol's terms (pattern literals of every step, choose values, domain and domains values, fresh values) and marks by name every atom or symmetric key whose name, with a session suffix #k removed, ends in _secr. The secrets argument is an array of at most 16 terms read by boundedTerm; a listed term none of whose atoms (Knowledge atoms, keys and agent names) occurs among the protocol's terms is rejected, naming the term. Marked and listed secrets are merged without duplicates, at most 16 in all. For each secret check_ctl decides {AG: {not: {knows: {actor: attacker, term}}}} over the same Protocol.draft as the formula: proved is kept, disproved is leaked (with its evidence and Protocol.replayWire), anything else, a truncated model included, is resource-limit. ProtocolTool.protectors walks every send pattern of every declared role: for each enc whose body contains a secret (a literal holding the term, or a variable named _secr, or a variable a choose binds to a secret), the protector is the key pattern's opening key (a literal's Msg.inverse, pk(a) gives sk(a), a variable its own name), reported with the role and step. ProtocolTool.lint reports names of atoms, keys and variables containing passw, secret, token, priv or cred (case-insensitive) that are not secrets. With secrets, the formula is optional; without a formula, the verdict is disproved if any secret leaked, resource-limit if any is resource-limit, proved otherwise; a formula keeps its own verdict and the secrets come beside it. Neither a formula nor a secret is an input error. checkProtocol is checkProtocolWith bounds.maxModelNodes, the bound being a parameter only so the cut-off answer can be tested on a small model."
      ["LeanFM/SecretsTests.lean: a password login that sends hpasswd_secr = H(passwd_secr) in clear keeps passwd_secr and leaks the marked hash, with the replay on the wire; the same login under pk(Server) keeps both and names sk(Server) as the protector; a listed {hash: {atom: passwd_secr}} behaves like the named one; a listed term whose atoms are not in the protocol is rejected; 17 secrets are rejected; a name such as password left unmarked is a warning; no formula and no secret is rejected; a model cut off at the state bound (checkProtocolWith with a bound of 3; the tool's is 4096) reports resource-limit for every secret, never kept", "LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: Needham-Schroeder with nb_secr marked and no formula: leaked in 1978 with the wire shown, and leaked with Lowe's fix too, because Bob may run with Ivo, which is why conditional secrecy stays a formula"]
  , decision "mcp-check-metrics"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.metrics", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety"]
      ["LeanFM/CheckMetrics.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "Server.lean"]
      "LeanFM.CheckMetrics keeps a pure Stats: per (tool, verdict) a call count and duration, states and transitions histograms, per tool bound hits and a secrets histogram. Stats.record takes the tool name, the list of listed tool names, the tool's Except String Json result and the elapsed nanoseconds; the tool label is the name if listed, else other; the verdict label is the result's verdict field if it is in a fixed list (proved, disproved, resource-limit, unknown, unsupported, met, violated, undecided, open, ok, issues, conforms, violates, clean, findings, generated, answered, compatible, incompatible, running, finished, stuck, kept, leaked), else other, and rejected for an error; states and transitions are read only as numbers from the result's states and transitions fields; secrets as the length of a secrets array. Histograms use fixed buckets (seconds: 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 60; states and transitions: 10, 100, 1000, 10000, 100000, 1000000; secrets: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16) and render cumulative le buckets, +Inf, _sum and _count with HELP and TYPE lines. A process-wide IO.Ref (initialize checkStats) holds it. MCP's tools/call measures toolResult with IO.monoNanosNow and modifies the ref after the result is computed, so recording cannot change or fail the answer. Server's /metrics appends Stats.render to the existing static metrics."
      ["LeanFM/CheckMetricsTests.lean: an unlisted tool is other; an unknown verdict is other; an error is rejected; histogram buckets are cumulative and end at +Inf with _count; a recorded check_protocol answer whose roles, terms and secrets have distinctive names leaves none of them in the rendered text; every rendered line is a comment or name{labels} value", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: after a session_sla call over JSON-RPC the metrics count it under tool session_sla and verdict met"]
  , decision "logic-reassembly"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reassembly", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Reassembly.lean"]
      "LeanFM.Session.Reassembly: a stream position holds empty, right (the signature's byte) or wrong (another byte); a State is the defender's and the host's buffers plus alerted and delivered. Config.segments lists every (offset, run of right/wrong bytes); Config.step applies a segment to the defender with its policy (first keeps a filled position, last overwrites), sets alerted when the defender's buffer is all right, and applies it to the host unless a blocking defender has alerted; delivered is set when the host's buffer is all right. A blocked or delivered state has no successors. Config.explore is breadth-first with a hash index up to 4096 states, one edge per pair of states, and marks truncation; check_reassembly answers resource-limit when truncated. Config.draft writes a check_ctl draft whose transitions carry the claims alerted(sig) and delivered(sig), splitting a segment that makes both through a midpoint, and declares both claims on an unreachable state. Defaults: signature EVIL, box first, host last, blocking, formula never delivered (blocking) or never (delivered and not alerted) (passive). Signatures longer than 4 characters or containing . are rejected, as are unknown policies and modes. The answer keeps check_ctl's verdict, reading, mode and evidence, and adds the segments of the evidence path as {offset, data} with . for a wrong byte, both policies and a note."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: first/last and last/first are evaded, with the two-segment evasions (a decoy byte then the signature over it; the first byte then a decoy over it); first/first and last/last are proved; passive is evaded; long signatures, a . in the signature, unknown policies and modes are rejected", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: check_reassembly is listed and answers over JSON-RPC with segments"]
  , decision "logic-knows-atom"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.knows", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "A formula atom {knows: {actor, term}} names an actor declared in the draft's knowledge field {actor: {initial: [terms], seesAll}}; an undeclared actor is an input error, like an unknown state. A draft transition may carry a term. A model node is (task state, message just taken, each declared actor's knowledge), the knowledge taken apart by Knowledge.learn and kept in one order, so equal knowledge compares equal; a transition's term reaches an actor that is its from or to, or every actor that sees all channels. The atom holds at a node when Knowledge.knows decides the term from that node's knowledge. Path evidence lists what each declared actor holds at each step. Canonical tasks declare no knowledge."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: in a draft of Lowe's attack, never(Bob finished with Alice and the network knows nb) is disproved with a path whose last step shows nb held; with Lowe's message it is proved; one state reached with and without a leak is two nodes, so AG and EF disagree there; an undeclared actor is rejected; a formula without knowledge atoms gives the same verdicts as before"]
  , decision "logic-evidence"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Return evidence as paths of {state, message} steps. Holding existential claims (EF, EX, EU, EG, EW, stronglyImplies) return a witness: breadth-first shortest path for reachability claims and a lasso (prefix plus loop start) for EG. Failing universal claims (AG, never, AX, AF, AU, AW) return a counterexample computed as the witness of the dual existential. A proved universal claim reports the number of reachable nodes checked. A failing stronglyImplies says whether the antecedent is unreachable (vacuous) or the implication fails."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: counterexample for AG not after(LogicInputRejected) on mcp_logic_consult ends with LogicInputRejected; lasso for AF on a draft cycle; witness for EF terminal"]
  , decision "logic-explain"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.explain"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "explain_formula returns the English reading, the negation pushed through duals, the requirement mode (always, never, eventually, possibly or compound), typing errors, unknown atoms when an optional task is supplied, and lints: AG over implies suggests stronglyImplies; AF AG and AG AF are each read explicitly; an optional English sentence containing always/never/every/must with an existential formula, or can/may/possible with a universal one, is flagged as a likely quantifier mismatch."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: reading and negation of AG(implies(after M, AF terminal)); stronglyImplies lint; sentence/quantifier mismatch lint"]
  , decision "logic-trace"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.trace"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "check_trace accepts at most 512 envelopes {id, prevIds, causeIds, task, src, dst, message}. Report duplicate ids, dangling references, references to later events (a cause must precede its effect in the submitted order), cycles, src/dst contradicting the canonical message schema, concurrent pairs (unordered under the transitive closure of prev and cause edges, listing at most 64), and per task whether the events in submitted order are accepted by that task's typed transitions from its initial state, or the first rejected event. Return a Mermaid sequenceDiagram and a Mermaid causal flowchart (prev solid, cause dashed) generated from the events."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: valid mcp_logic_consult trace accepted with diagram; duplicate, dangling, forward cause, wrong src and out-of-grammar event each reported; two independent requests reported concurrent"]
  , decision "logic-observability"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.observability", "proof:Hidden predicates never pass as observable"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "classify_requirement takes a formula or a list of predicate texts and an optional task. at, after, terminal and field atoms are observable once they name a declared state or message; derived(name) atoms are derived and name the reducer or policy declaration that must exist; predicate text is never observable: it is derived when it mentions a declared message or state (needs a reducer over those events) and implementation-only otherwise, with the advice to move it to Implementation.lean or restate it over events and a proposed event name, actor and fields. check_ctl treats predicate and derived atoms as unknown, never as true or false."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: 'cache is consistent' is implementation-only with a proposed event; 'LogicVerdict count' is derived; no predicate text is ever observable; check_ctl with a predicate atom never proves"]
  , decision "logic-mcp-wiring"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reference", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.knows", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reassembly", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "task:mcp_logic_consult", "message:LogicConsultRequested", "message:LogicVerdict", "message:LogicInputRejected", "property:Every consultation ends"]
      ["LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean"]
      "Add the leanfm://language/logic-reference markdown resource. Give tools an inputSchema; list explain_formula, check_ctl, check_trace, classify_requirement, knows, check_protocol and check_reassembly. Pass params.arguments to toolResult. A LogicVerdict is a tool result whose structuredContent holds {tool, verdict, reading, evidence, bounds} and whose single text block is a one-line summary (tool, verdict, reading) of at most 400 characters, never a second copy of the answer; LogicInputRejected is a tool result with isError true, structuredContent {tool, verdict: rejected, reason} and the summary line with the reason. knows answers as decision logic-knows specifies. The tools remain read-only, deterministic and path-free; no tool changes project state."
      ["LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: tools/list includes the logic tools with schemas; check_ctl over JSON-RPC returns a counterexample; malformed input returns isError; knows over JSON-RPC returns a derivation; check_protocol is listed and answers over JSON-RPC; logic-reference resource readable"]
  , decision "logic-knows"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.knows", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Knowledge.lean"]
      "Terms are LeanFM.Session.Knowledge.Msg, parsed from JSON objects with exactly one key (atom, key, pk, sk: a string; pair: two terms; tuple: two or more terms, nested to the right; enc: {key, body}; hash: a term) within the formula bounds: 256 nodes, depth 64. Envelopes are parsed like check_trace events with a term instead of a message, at most 512. The causal past of at is a backward search over prevIds and causeIds; visible events are those whose src or dst is the actor, or all of them when seesAll is true. Analysis keeps each piece with the id of the event that first gave it (initial for the actor's starting terms) and how it was obtained, adding the parts of every held piece (Knowledge.synth on the held set decides whether an opening key can be built) until a round adds nothing. A query is decided by Knowledge.synth on the held pieces, the decision proved sound by knows_sound; proved carries the derivation as a tree whose leaves are held pieces and whose inner nodes are pair, encrypt or hash; disproved carries an exhaustive note that the analysis reached its fixpoint. Without a query the verdict is derived and the answer is the held pieces."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: in Lowe's attack the network does not hold nb before Alice answers, and does after, with a derivation naming the delivering event; an event concurrent with at never contributes; a signature opens with the public key; a hash is never inverted; a public-key ciphertext stays closed without the private key; malformed terms, unknown at and dangling references are rejected"]
  , decision "logic-view"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.view", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "clause:selfhost.mcp.readonly", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/MCPView.lean", "LeanFM/MCPView.html"]
      "LeanFM/MCPView.html is one self-contained document (inline style and script; its only URL is the SVG namespace) compiled into the server with include_str as LeanFM.MCP.logicViewHtml. LeanFM.MCP.logicViewUri is ui://leanfm/logic-view; resources/list lists it with mimeType text/html;profile=mcp-app, and resources/read returns it as text with _meta.ui {csp: {connectDomains: [], resourceDomains: [], frameDomains: [], baseUriDomains: []}, prefersBorder: true}; resourceContents answers no other ui:// URI. The four tools gain _meta {ui: {resourceUri: logicViewUri}} in tools/list; their results are unchanged (one text summary and structuredContent). The script speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over window.parent.postMessage: it requests ui/initialize with appInfo, appCapabilities {availableDisplayModes: [inline]} and protocolVersion 2026-01-26, retrying with backoff for 30 s and showing an error in the page if no answer comes; it then notifies ui/notifications/initialized; it renders the params of ui/notifications/tool-result (structuredContent, or the text content when there is none), follows the theme in hostContext and ui/notifications/host-context-changed, and reports its height with ui/notifications/size-changed. Rendering builds nodes with document.createElement, createElementNS and textContent only, and draws at most 64 path steps and 256 knowledge items, counting the rest. The view is dark by default."
      ["LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: tools/list gives check_protocol, check_ctl, check_reassembly and knows _meta.ui.resourceUri ui://leanfm/logic-view and no other tool; resources/list includes the view with mimeType text/html;profile=mcp-app; resources/read returns the HTML with empty CSP domain lists; the HTML contains no URL but the SVG namespace, no src= or href= attribute, no fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, EventSource or import(, and no innerHTML, outerHTML, insertAdjacentHTML, document.write, eval or new Function; an unknown ui:// URI is rejected; a check_protocol result is still one text item plus structuredContent"]
  , decision "mcp-hosting"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.hosting", "clause:selfhost.mcp.transport", "clause:selfhost.mcp.readonly", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety"]
      ["LeanFM/MCPHost.lean", "McpServer.lean", "lakefile.toml", "deploy/mcp/Dockerfile", "deploy/mcp/README.md"]
      "LeanFM.MCPHost holds the hosted endpoint as functions of a parsed request (method, path, headers, body, client key), testable without a socket. HostConfig.fromEnv reads LEANFM_MCP_BIND (default 127.0.0.1), LEANFM_MCP_PORT (default 8090), LEANFM_MCP_ORIGINS (comma-separated, default https://claude.ai,https://claude.com), LEANFM_MCP_TOKEN (optional bearer token), LEANFM_MCP_RATE and LEANFM_MCP_BURST (requests per minute and burst per client, default 60 and 20), LEANFM_MCP_MAX_INFLIGHT (default 4) and LEANFM_MCP_TRUST_PROXY (use the first X-Forwarded-For address as the client key; default off, so the socket peer is the key). MCPHost.gate decides, in order: 404 for any path but /mcp and /healthz; 403 for an Origin outside the allowlist; OPTIONS /mcp is a 204 preflight; 405 with Allow: POST, OPTIONS for other methods on /mcp; 413 over 256 KiB; 401 when a token is configured and Authorization is not Bearer followed by it (compared in constant time); 415 unless Content-Type is application/json; 406 unless Accept is absent or admits application/json, */* or application/*; 400 for an Mcp-Protocol-Version other than 2025-03-26, 2025-06-18 or 2025-11-25. CORS headers are added to every response to an allowed Origin. MCPHost.handle then applies a token bucket per client key (integer units: capacity burst times 60000, refill rate per millisecond, cost 60000; the table is cleared when it exceeds 10,000 keys) and a counter of in-flight tools/call requests, calls LeanFM.MCP.dispatch, answers 202 with no body when the message has no id, and passes the reply through compactReply: when the compressed JSON exceeds 100,000 characters it removes holds from every evidence path step and adds a note, then keeps only the last 64 path steps with a note, and otherwise replaces the reply with JSON-RPC error -32000 naming the limit. Every response carries Cache-Control: no-store and X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. McpServer.lean (exe leanfm-mcp) reads one request per connection with a 15-second deadline using ChatUI.HttpRequest.frameLength? and the 256 KiB body limit, takes the socket peer as the client key, calls MCPHost.handle and logs one line per request: method, path, JSON-RPC method, tool name, status and milliseconds. It links LeanFM.MCP and nothing from the chat UI server. deploy/mcp holds a Dockerfile that builds leanfm-mcp and runs it on 0.0.0.0 behind the platform's TLS, and a README covering TLS, origins, rate limits, the directory's requirements and that OAuth is not yet implemented."
      ["LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: preflight from https://claude.ai is 204 with that origin and the MCP headers allowed and exposed; a POST from an unlisted origin is 403 with no CORS headers; a POST with no Origin is served; GET /mcp is 405; /api/llm, /api/session, / and /renders/ are 404; non-JSON Content-Type is 415; Accept text/html is 406; Mcp-Protocol-Version 1999-01-01 is 400; a 300 KiB body is 413; with a token configured, no token and a wrong token are 401 and the right token is served; a notification is 202 with no body; the burst is served and the next request is 429 with Retry-After; a tools/call beyond the in-flight limit is 503; an oversized reply is compacted below the limit keeping its verdict, and an irreducible one becomes error -32000; /healthz is ok; no response sets Access-Control-Allow-Origin to * or Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "leanfm-mcp started locally answers a curl preflight and a tools/call (checked by hand during implementation)"]
  , decision "logic-book"
      ["output:selfhost.mcp.logic.interaction", "output:selfhost.mcp.logic.states"]
      ["book/chapters/mcp-tools.tex", "book/generated/mcp-logic-examples.tex", "LogicInteractionExample.lean"]
